# ==============================================================================
# SansKrypt / Major Project .gitignore
# ==============================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. SENSITIVE / PRIVATE CREDENTIALS & SECRETS
# Why: .env files contain private API keys (e.g. GEMINI_API_KEY, SECRET_KEY)
# and secrets. Committing these to a public/private GitHub repo exposes keys to abuse.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
*.env
*.pem
*.key
*.cert

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. MACHINE LEARNING MODELS & LARGE BINARIES (>100MB GitHub limit)
# Why: Fine-tuned model checkpoints (such as models/sanskrypt_translator/pytorch_model.bin)
# exceed 300+ MB. GitHub rejects single file pushes > 100 MB and has repo limits.
# Model weights should be stored via Git LFS, Hugging Face Hub, or trained locally.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
models/
*.bin
*.pt
*.pth
*.onnx
*.safetensors
*.h5
*.tflite
*.ckpt

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. USER UPLOADS & RUNTIME GENERATED FILES
# Why: Uploaded manuscript images and preprocessed images are dynamic runtime
# data and can be private. Keeping them out prevents bloating repo size.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. RUNTIME DATABASES & LOCAL STORAGE
# Why: SQLite database files (*.db, *.sqlite, *.sqlite3) store runtime data,
# user records, and binary state. Committing databases leads to merge conflicts
# and may leak user-submitted manuscripts or personal metadata.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
sanskrypt.db

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. PYTHON BYTECODE & COMPILATION CACHES
# Why: Compiled Python files (.pyc, __pycache__) are platform-dependent artifacts
# generated automatically at runtime and should not be tracked by version control.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
build/
develop-eggs/
dist/
downloads/
eggs/
.eggs/
lib/
lib64/
parts/
sdist/
var/
wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. TESTING, COVERAGE & LINTING CACHES
# Why: Pytest and coverage caches are temporary local performance caches.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
.cache
nosetests.xml
coverage.xml
*.cover
*.py,cover
.hypothesis/
htmlcov/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENTS
# Why: Virtual environments contain hundreds of MBs of third-party dependencies
# specific to your local OS/Python setup. Dependencies should be installed from
# requirements.txt instead.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
venv/
.venv/
env/
ENV/
env.bak/
venv.bak/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. LEGACY / NESTED SUB-REPOSITORIES
# Why: The nested SansKrypt/ folder has its own .git and outdated prototype code.
# Ignoring it prevents Git submodule conflicts and redundant files during push.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SansKrypt/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. IDE, EDITOR & OS METADATA
# Why: System-generated hidden files and IDE workspace settings differ per developer.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
